{
  "case": "latest signup date",
  "variant": "with",
  "trial": 0,
  "run": {
    "final_text": "I wrote `latest.py`. When run, it prints `2025-10-12`, the signup date of the most recent user (Priya).\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nfrom acme_client import Client\n\nusers = Client().list_users()\nlatest = max(u[\"created_at\"] for u in users)  # Unix timestamp in seconds\nprint(datetime.fromtimestamp(latest, tz=timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\"))\n```\n\nThe `acme-client-guide` skill says `created_at` is in milliseconds, but the `acme_client.py` in this directory stores it in seconds (for example, `1760227200` is 2025-10-12). If I'd followed the skill and divided by 1000, the script would have printed a date in January 1970. The script uses seconds instead. If the real Acme API sends milliseconds, the division needs to go back in, and the skill or the local client should be updated so they agree.",
